[Start "short-system 1...c6"]

2. c4 d5 3. exd5 ( 3. cxd5 cxd5 4. exd5 ( 4. e5 Bf5 { The bishop gets out before ...e6, as in the Advance Variation. White's pawn on e5 has no partner on d4 yet. } ) ( 4. Nc3 dxe4 5. Nxe4 Nf6 ) 4... Nf6 ) 3... cxd5 { Recaptures with the pawn, so Black keeps a pawn in the centre and the c-file opens. } 4. cxd5 ( 4. d4 Nf6 ) ( 4. c5 e5 { White's pawn on c5 has left the centre, so Black takes it over. } ) 4... Nf6 { Wins the pawn on d5 back and develops with tempo. } 5. Nc3 ( 5. Bc4 Nxd5 ) ( 5. Bb5+ Nbd7 { Blocks with the knight, which can take back on d5 next. } ) ( 5. Nf3 Nxd5 ) ( 5. d4 { The same position as the Panov Attack after 4.c4 Nf6 5.cxd5: Black takes back on d5. } ) 5... Nxd5 { Takes back the pawn. The knight is well placed in the centre, where it attacks the knight on c3. } 6. Nf3 ( 6. Nxd5 Qxd5 { The queen is safe in the centre: no White piece can attack it quickly. Black follows with ...Nc6 and ...Bf5 or ...e5. } 7. d4 Nc6 ) ( 6. Bc4 Nb6 { Hits the bishop and keeps the knights on the board. } ) ( 6. Bb5+ Nc6 7. Nf3 { The same position as the main line. } ) ( 6. d4 Nc6 ) Nc6 { Develops and adds pressure on d4 and e5. } 7. Bb5 e6 { Supports the knight on d5 and prepares to develop the bishop. } 8. O-O Be7 { Gets ready to castle. } 9. d4 O-O { King safe. Black has a healthy position and nothing to worry about. The knight on d5 is strong, so aim at the pawns with ...Bd7 and ...Rc8. If White varies, castle and play ...Bd7. } *
